---
import BaseLayoutV2 from '../layouts/BaseLayoutV2.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';
import {
  grid, checks, check, composite, goCase, directionByCase, dates, refresh,
  prereg, parity, deviations, cleanItems, limitations, fmt, isDefinitional,
} from '../lib/benchmark';

const g = grid();
const d = dates();
const r = refresh();
const p = prereg();
const comp = composite();
const gc = goCase();
const dbc = directionByCase();

const openai = r.legs.find((l) => l.vendor === 'OpenAI')!;
const anthropic = r.legs.find((l) => l.vendor === 'Anthropic')!;

const c6 = check('c6');
const c7 = check('c7');

// One cell per run. Grouped 3 / 6 / 6 to show the case shape of the grid.
const caseSizes = g.cases.map((c) => c.n_per_arm);

const artefacts = [
  { title: 'Case A, Meridian', sub: 'The frozen prompt and fact sheet.', path: 'evidence/preregistration/v3/cases/case-A-anchor-client.md' },
  { title: 'Case B, Larkfield', sub: 'The GO case, and the case where the file adds least.', path: 'evidence/preregistration/v3/cases/case-B-go-case.md' },
  { title: 'Case C, Corvan', sub: 'Adapted from a documented Australian post mortem.', path: 'evidence/preregistration/v3/cases/case-C-disguised-real.md' },
  { title: 'Test protocol', sub: 'Conditions, the answering rule, the target grid and the scoring procedure.', path: 'evidence/preregistration/v3/protocol.md' },
  { title: 'Scoring rubric', sub: 'The pass criteria for each of the eight checks.', path: 'evidence/preregistration/v3/rubric-operationalisations.md' },
  { title: 'Pre-registration record', sub: 'The hashes, and this record’s own edit history.', path: 'evidence/preregistration/v3/PREREGISTRATION.md' },
];
---
<BaseLayoutV2
  title="Benchmark | GreenSquare AI"
  description={`A ${g.runs_total} run pre-registered benchmark of The Decision across two AI models, three business cases and three conditions, with the method, the results, every deviation and the limitations stated plainly.`}
  current="benchmark"
>
<div class="dl">

  <!-- 1. CLAIM -->
  <section class="dl-s dl-s--open">
    <div class="dl-w">
      <h1 class="dl-h1">The file asked its own questions, obtained the decisive facts, and surfaced the hidden decision in {fmt(c6.armC).text} runs. It started from a short question with no facts supplied.</h1>

      <p class="dl-lead">The informed baseline reached {fmt(c6.armB).text} on the same check, and it was handed the complete fact sheet before it answered. Those two numbers are the finding. What the file closes is the gap between typing a short question and holding a complete fact sheet.</p>

      <div class="dl-panel">
        <p class="dl-panel__k">Why that is a fair comparison</p>
        <p>The pre-registration fixed this before any run. The informed condition carries <em>equal information to the loaded condition; the only difference is the product's discipline</em>, and it names that comparison the sceptic's controlled one. In the loaded condition the operator answers each question <em>using only the fact sheet</em> and replies <em>that has not been assessed</em> to anything off-sheet, so the operator cannot feed the model convenient answers. The frozen prompt is identical across all three conditions.</p>
        <p class="dl-src">Source: <code>protocol.md:10-13</code>, hashed and frozen on {p.authoredAt}.</p>
      </div>

      <p>The question-only condition scored {fmt(c6.armA).text} on the same check, and that zero is not evidence of anything. The hidden decision in each case turns on three facts placed in the fact sheet. None appears in the frozen prompt, and a single-turn condition receives nothing else, so it cannot obtain them. All three case files were checked. The zero restates the design of the test. It was previously the headline number of this benchmark and it should never have been used that way.</p>

      <p><strong>The Decision Brief is not on sale.</strong> The launch edition is in preparation. The only thing available from this page today is the free Decision Frame. What was tested is a file fixed by the SHA-256 hash published with the pre-registration, and nothing here commits the launch edition to being that same file.</p>

      <ul class="dl-status">
        <li>{g.runs_total} runs</li>
        <li>{g.n_per_arm} per condition</li>
        <li>{g.cases.length} cases</li>
        <li>{g.models.length} models</li>
        <li>Scored {d.scoredOn}</li>
        <li>Adjudicated {d.adjudicatedOn}</li>
        <li>Both models superseded</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. THE COMPARISON -->
  <section class="dl-s">
    <div class="dl-w">
      <p class="dl-k">The comparison</p>
      <h2 class="dl-h2">The comparison is against an operator who has already pasted in every fact.</h2>
      <p>The objection this page has to answer is not about someone typing one lazy question. It is about you, doing your job, pasting in what you already know. That person is the all-facts condition, and it is the condition this page reports hardest.</p>

      <ol class="dl-arms">
        <li><span class="dl-ix">/A</span><div><h3>Question only</h3><p>The frozen prompt only. One turn. If the model asks questions, they are not answered.</p></div></li>
        <li><span class="dl-ix">/B</span><div><h3>All facts</h3><p>The same prompt with the full fact sheet appended in the first message. One turn.</p></div></li>
        <li><span class="dl-ix">/C</span><div><h3>The Decision loaded</h3><p>The file, then the same prompt. The model interrogates. The operator answers only from the fact sheet.</p></div></li>
      </ol>

      <p>The loaded condition and the question-only condition start from the same input. The all-facts condition does not. So the comparison that carries the product claim is the loaded condition against the all-facts condition on the result, and against the question-only condition on the starting position.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. RESULT IN ONE VIEW -->
  <section class="dl-s dl-s--fill">
    <div class="dl-w">
      <p class="dl-k">The result</p>
      <h2 class="dl-h2">The informed baseline tied the loaded file on four of the eight checks and scored higher on one.</h2>

      <figure class="dl-fig">
        <figcaption class="dl-fig__read">Surfacing the hidden decision. The all-facts condition was handed the facts. The loaded condition had to ask for them.</figcaption>
        <div class="dl-mx" role="img" aria-labelledby="m1d">
          {(['armA','armB','armC'] as const).map((arm) => {
            const cell = c6[arm]; const def = isDefinitional(c6, arm);
            return (
              <div class="dl-mx__row">
                <p class="dl-mx__lab">{arm === 'armA' ? 'Question only' : arm === 'armB' ? 'All facts' : 'The Decision loaded'}</p>
                <div class="dl-strip" aria-hidden="true">
                  {caseSizes.map((size, ci) => (
                    <div class="dl-grp">
                      {Array.from({ length: size }).map((_, i) => {
                        const idx = caseSizes.slice(0, ci).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) + i;
                        return <span class={`dl-c ${idx < cell.met ? 'is-met' : def ? 'is-def' : ''}`}></span>;
                      })}
                    </div>
                  ))}
                </div>
                <p class="dl-mx__n"><b>{cell.met}</b> / {cell.n}{def === 'full' ? <em> definitional</em> : null}</p>
              </div>
            );
          })}
        </div>
        <p id="m1d" class="dl-sr">Question only, {fmt(c6.armA).text}, definitional. All facts, {fmt(c6.armB).text}. The Decision loaded, {fmt(c6.armC).text}.</p>
        <p class="dl-src">One cell per run, grouped by case, {caseSizes.join(' / ')}. Fill shows the count, not which specific runs passed. Source: adjudicated grid, {d.adjudicatedOn}.</p>
      </figure>

      <div class="dl-note">
        <p>{c6.definitional_note}</p>
      </div>

      <figure class="dl-fig">
        <figcaption class="dl-fig__read">Producing a defensible brief, the composite of five process checks.</figcaption>
        <div class="dl-mx" role="img" aria-labelledby="m2d">
          {(['armA','armB','armC'] as const).map((arm) => (
            <div class="dl-mx__row">
              <p class="dl-mx__lab">{arm === 'armA' ? 'Question only' : arm === 'armB' ? 'All facts' : 'The Decision loaded'}</p>
              <div class="dl-strip" aria-hidden="true">
                {caseSizes.map((size, ci) => (
                  <div class="dl-grp">
                    {Array.from({ length: size }).map((_, i) => {
                      const idx = caseSizes.slice(0, ci).reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) + i;
                      return <span class={`dl-c ${idx < comp[arm].met ? 'is-met' : ''}`}></span>;
                    })}
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
              <p class="dl-mx__n"><b>{comp[arm].met}</b> / {comp[arm].n}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p id="m2d" class="dl-sr">Question only, {fmt(comp.armA).text}. All facts, {fmt(comp.armB).text}. The Decision loaded, {fmt(comp.armC).text}.</p>
        <p class="dl-src">Derived: the logical AND of {comp.components.length} process checks. Not an independent result, and not the pre-registered headline. Source: adjudicated grid, {d.adjudicatedOn}.</p>
      </figure>

      <div class="dl-note">
        <p>This composite is gated by a definitional cell. A single-turn condition cannot ask before answering. Recomputed over the four remaining checks the figures do not move, at {comp.recomputed_without_c1.armA} of {g.n_per_arm}, {comp.recomputed_without_c1.armB} of {g.n_per_arm} and {comp.recomputed_without_c1.armC} of {g.n_per_arm}, because the question-only condition compared real options in {fmt(check('c2').armA).text} runs and the all-facts condition tagged its evidence in {fmt(check('c3').armB).text}. That recomputation was done after the fact and was not pre-registered.</p>
        <p>What the composite shows is discipline, not reasoning. Asking first, comparing options and tagging evidence are three behaviours the file instructs the model to perform, and they are the three that separate cleanly. The section below sets out the evidence against a reasoning claim, and it is substantial.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. WHERE THE BASELINE WINS -->
  <section class="dl-s">
    <div class="dl-w">
      <p class="dl-k">Against the product</p>
      <h2 class="dl-h2">Where the informed baseline matched or beat the loaded file.</h2>

      <p>On surfacing the hidden decision, the check the rubric calls the core of the benchmark, the informed baseline scored {fmt(c6.armB).text} and the loaded file {fmt(c6.armC).text}. The informed baseline is above the loaded file.</p>

      <p>On stating what would change the call, all three conditions scored {fmt(check('c5').armC).text}. That check does not discriminate at all. On naming an untested constraint, the informed baseline and the loaded file both scored {fmt(check('c4').armC).text}. On catching the planted bias, both scored {fmt(check('c8').armC).text}.</p>

      <p><strong>The direction split by case is the most adverse figure on this page.</strong> Across Cases A and C, every condition scored {dbc.cases_A_and_C.armA.met} of {dbc.cases_A_and_C.armA.n}. Outside Case B, direction did not discriminate between a short question and the loaded file at all. The whole measured deficit sits in Case B, where the question-only condition scored {fmt(dbc.case_B.armA).text}, and Case B is the case on which ChatGPT invoked live web search unprompted during those runs. They were not the clean no-context condition the protocol describes.</p>

      <p><strong>Case B is the case where the file adds least.</strong> The protocol required that case to be identified and it never has been until now. With every fact supplied, the informed baseline matched the loaded file exactly: {fmt(gc.armB).text} on direction against {fmt(gc.armC).text}, on both models. With the same qualifier this page applies to any favourable sentence: that is one case, n of {gc.armA.n} per condition, and the generalisation is weak in both directions.</p>

      <p>What the file changes is the input it can start from. The informed baseline reaches those results from a complete fact sheet. The loaded file reaches them from the short question, and it produced a brief with its options, evidence tags and change criteria attached in {fmt(comp.armC).text} runs, where the informed baseline produced one in {fmt(comp.armB).text}.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. PER CASE AND MODEL -->
  <section class="dl-s dl-s--fill">
    <div class="dl-w">
      <p class="dl-k">Grid shape</p>
      <h2 class="dl-h2">The grid is not balanced across cases or models.</h2>
      <p>{g.runs_total} runs, {g.n_per_arm} per condition. Case A ran on ChatGPT only, {g.cases[0].n_per_arm * 3} runs. Cases B and C ran on both models. Each condition's n of {g.n_per_arm} is {caseSizes.join(', ')} across the three cases, and Case A carries no Claude leg. Claude runs on Case A exist. They are design-phase pilot runs, excluded here, and deviation 2 sets out why.</p>
      <p>The rubric names per-case, per-condition counts as the headline unit. This page publishes two such cuts: Case B in full, and direction split by case. The other seven checks are still aggregate only, and the pre-registration required them per case. Until they are published, read every aggregate here as an average over an unbalanced grid.</p>
      <p>No per-model split is published. The counts pool {g.models.map((m) => m.name).join(' and ')}. The one per-model statement on this page is Case B, where the informed baseline and the loaded file tied on both.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. ONE CASE -->
  <section class="dl-s">
    <div class="dl-w">
      <p class="dl-k">One case</p>
      <h2 class="dl-h2">Larkfield is the case where the file adds least.</h2>
      <p>Case B is the GO case: the sealed key points to the more committing option rather than to caution. It was scored on direction across {gc.armA.n} runs per condition, three repeats on each of two models.</p>
      <p>Question only reached the key direction in {fmt(gc.armA).text} runs. All facts in {fmt(gc.armB).text}. Loaded in {fmt(gc.armC).text}. Supplying the fact sheet was sufficient on this case.</p>
      <p>The {fmt(gc.armA).text} carries the qualification above. ChatGPT invoked live web search unprompted during those runs, so that condition was not clean, and the whole of the direction deficit on this benchmark sits inside them.</p>
      <p>The two transcript excerpts previously shown here are withdrawn. One tagged a figure DERIVED where the case file supplies it directly, which under the product's own scheme makes it GIVEN, and the case file is one click away. The replacement will show the model integrating a fact it obtained by asking, rather than restating one it was given.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. ALL CHECKS -->
  <section class="dl-s dl-s--fill">
    <div class="dl-w">
      <p class="dl-k">Full grid</p>
      <h2 class="dl-h2">All eight checks, with every disputed cell adjudicated.</h2>
      <p>Counts are out of {g.n_per_arm} per condition, pooled across both models and all three cases. Five cells the two scoring passes disagreed on were adjudicated on {d.adjudicatedOn} against the sealed reference answers, whose SHA-256 was verified against the pre-registration table on both LF and CRLF line endings. The key was fixed before any run and has not been altered. No cell is withheld.</p>

      <div class="dl-tw">
        <table class="dl-t">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Check</th><th scope="col" class="n">Question only</th><th scope="col" class="n">All facts</th><th scope="col" class="n">Loaded</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {checks('process').map((c) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{c.page_label}</th>
                {(['armA','armB','armC'] as const).map((arm) => (
                  <td class="n">{c[arm].met} / {c[arm].n}{isDefinitional(c, arm) === 'full' ? <span class="dl-def">def</span> : isDefinitional(c, arm) === 'partial' ? <span class="dl-def">part</span> : null}</td>
                ))}
              </tr>
            ))}
            <tr class="dl-t__comp">
              <th scope="row">Composite, all five above</th>
              {(['armA','armB','armC'] as const).map((arm) => <td class="n">{comp[arm].met} / {comp[arm].n}</td>)}
            </tr>
            {checks('judgment').map((c) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">{c.page_label}</th>
                {(['armA','armB','armC'] as const).map((arm) => (
                  <td class="n">{c[arm].met} / {c[arm].n}{isDefinitional(c, arm) === 'full' ? <span class="dl-def">def</span> : isDefinitional(c, arm) === 'partial' ? <span class="dl-def">part</span> : null}</td>
                ))}
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="dl-src">def: the cell is an artefact of the test design, not a measurement. part: partly so.</p>

      <div class="dl-notes">
        {checks().filter((c) => c.definitional_note).map((c) => (
          <p><span class="dl-nk">{c.page_label}</span> {c.definitional_note}</p>
        ))}
        {checks().filter((c) => c.adjudication).map((c) => (
          <p><span class="dl-nk">{c.page_label}, adjudication</span> {c.adjudication}</p>
        ))}
        {checks().filter((c) => c.caveat).map((c) => (
          <p><span class="dl-nk">{c.page_label}, caveat</span> {c.caveat}</p>
        ))}
        <p><span class="dl-nk">Partial passes</span> This table shows full passes only. The protocol requires baseline partial counts reported prominently, per case, and the rubric describes the full versus partial distinction as the core of the benchmark. No partial count is published anywhere on this site, including here.</p>
        <p><span class="dl-nk">What the first three checks measure</span> Asking first, comparing options and tagging evidence are behaviours the file instructs the model to perform. They separate cleanly because the file mandates them. They are evidence that the file is followed, not evidence that the model reasons better.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. DEVIATIONS -->
  <section class="dl-s">
    <div class="dl-w">
      <p class="dl-k">Against ourselves</p>
      <h2 class="dl-h2">{deviations().length} departures from the pre-registration and the published record.</h2>
      <p>Ranked by how much damage each does when someone hostile finds it. None is softened and none has been moved to the bottom of the page.</p>
      <ol class="dl-dev">
        {deviations().map((dv) => (
          <li><span class="dl-ix">{String(dv.n).padStart(2, '0')}</span><div><h3>{dv.title}</h3><p>{dv.detail}</p></div></li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <div class="dl-note">
        <p>Two obligations were met exactly.</p>
        {cleanItems().map((t) => <p>{t}</p>)}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 9. LIMITATIONS -->
  <section class="dl-s dl-s--fill">
    <div class="dl-w">
      <p class="dl-k">Limits</p>
      <h2 class="dl-h2">What this benchmark cannot tell you.</h2>
      <ul class="dl-lim">{limitations().map((t) => <li>{t}</li>)}</ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 10. INSPECT -->
  <section class="dl-s">
    <div class="dl-w">
      <p class="dl-k">Verify</p>
      <h2 class="dl-h2">Inspect it yourself, starting with the commit that actually exists.</h2>
      <p>The pre-registration commit is <code>{p.full}</code>, dated {p.authoredAt}. If you tried to verify this benchmark before and could not, the reason is deviation 3. The commit ID published until now is in no ref in the repository, and the procedure failed at its first step.</p>
      <p>Six inputs were hashed and frozen before the first scored run, and all six SHA-256 values match their files when read with LF line endings. If you hash with CRLF you will get six different values, for a reason that has nothing to do with this benchmark.</p>
      <p><strong>There is a limit to what you can check today, and it is a large one.</strong> No transcript is published. The sealed reference answers are not published. Nobody outside GreenSquare AI can re-score a single run or confirm a single adjudicated cell. Those are deviations 4 and 5.</p>
      <ul class="dl-art">
        {artefacts.map((a) => (
          <li><a href={p.fileUrl(a.path)} target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span><b>{a.title}</b><em>{a.sub}</em></span><span class="dl-art__go">View</span></a></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="dl-src">Every link is pinned to commit {p.short}, not to the current branch, so a later edit cannot change what you verified.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 11. STATUS -->
  <section class="dl-s dl-s--fill">
    <div class="dl-w">
      <p class="dl-k">Status</p>
      <h2 class="dl-h2">The refresh commitment has lapsed on both model legs.</h2>
      <p>GreenSquare AI commits to re-running this benchmark within {r.commitmentDays} days of a major model release. The grid was scored on {d.scoredOn} against {g.models.map((m) => m.name).join(' and ')}. Both have since been superseded and both windows have expired.</p>
      {r.legs.map((leg) => (
        <p><strong>The {leg.vendor} leg.</strong> {leg.tested_model} was superseded by {leg.superseded_by} on {leg.releasedOn}, {leg.daysSinceRelease} days before {d.currentAsAt}. The {r.commitmentDays} day window expired on {leg.dueOn}. As at {d.currentAsAt} the re-run is {leg.daysPastDue} days past that deadline. {leg.caveat}</p>
      ))}
      <p>No re-run date is published. Until one is, every figure on this page is a measurement of {g.models.map((m) => m.name).join(' and ')} as at {d.scoredOn} and should be read as historical.</p>
      <p>Benchmark figures have been removed from the other pages of this site rather than updated, so this page is the only record.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 12. NEXT -->
  <section class="dl-s dl-s--end">
    <div class="dl-w">
      <h2 class="dl-h2">Run the Decision Frame on a decision you are holding now.</h2>
      <p>The free Decision Frame is the structure without the engine: the first questions, in order, on one page. Run it against something live and see whether the questions change the call. That is a cheaper test of this page's claim than reading the table again.</p>
      <p class="dl-actions">
        <a class="dl-btn" href={withBase('/decision-frame/')}>Get the free Decision Frame</a>
        <a class="dl-link" href={withBase('/methodology/')}>Read the method and its caveats in full</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>
</BaseLayoutV2>

<style is:global>
  /* GreenSquare Design Language, scoped to this page's main element.
     The nav and footer remain on the v2 token system. */
  .dl {
    --ink: #131815; --paper: #F8F8F4; --white: #FFFFFF;
    --structure: #D6DAD5; --graphite: #5D645D;
    --green: #006142; --pale: #D8EAE1; --amber: #A16702;
    --f: 'Manrope', ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
    --m: 'IBM Plex Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
    background: var(--paper); color: var(--ink); font-family: var(--f);
    display: block;
  }
  .dl :where(p, li) { color: var(--ink); line-height: 1.62; }
  .dl code { font-family: var(--m); font-size: .86em; word-break: break-all; }
  .dl em { font-style: italic; }

  .dl-s { padding-block: clamp(3.25rem, 6vw, 5.5rem); border-top: 1px solid var(--structure); }
  .dl-s--open { border-top: 0; padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem); }
  .dl-s--fill { background: var(--white); }
  .dl-s--end { background: var(--white); }
  .dl-w { width: min(53rem, calc(100% - 2.5rem)); margin-inline: auto; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.15rem; }
  /* Flex children default to min-width:auto, which stops an overflow-x container
     from ever shrinking and pushes the whole page sideways instead. */
  .dl-w > * { min-width: 0; }

  .dl-h1 { font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.4vw, 2.4rem); line-height: 1.16; letter-spacing: -.02em; font-weight: 600; max-width: 26ch; margin: 0; text-wrap: balance; }
  .dl-h2 { font-size: clamp(1.18rem, 2.1vw, 1.5rem); line-height: 1.24; letter-spacing: -.015em; font-weight: 600; max-width: 34ch; margin: 0; text-wrap: balance; }
  .dl-lead { font-size: 1.08rem; }
  .dl p { margin: 0; }
  .dl-k { font-family: var(--m); font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .02em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--graphite); margin: 0; }

  .dl-panel { background: var(--pale); padding: 1.35rem 1.5rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .6rem; }
  .dl-panel__k { font-family: var(--m); font-size: .72rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .02em; color: var(--green); }
  .dl-src { font-family: var(--m); font-size: .74rem; color: var(--graphite); line-height: 1.55; }

  .dl-status { list-style: none; padding: .85rem 0 0; margin: .35rem 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--structure); display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .3rem 1.4rem; font-family: var(--m); font-size: .74rem; color: var(--graphite); }

  .dl-arms, .dl-dev { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: .4rem 0 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .dl-arms > li, .dl-dev > li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 3rem 1fr; gap: 1rem; padding: 1.05rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--structure); }
  /* Grid children also default to min-width:auto. Deviation 3 quotes a 40 character
     commit hash, which is one unbreakable word and will widen the page without this. */
  .dl-arms > li > *, .dl-dev > li > * { min-width: 0; }
  .dl-dev p, .dl-lim li, .dl code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
